.TH GENERATE_PDF_MAP 1 "September 2026" "Dragon GL" "Dragon GL DM Tools"
.SH NAME
generate_pdf_map \- render a Dragon GL floor dump as a faithful color PDF map
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B generate_pdf_map.py
.I dump
.I output_pdf
.RI [ floor_number ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B generate_pdf_map.py
converts a floor dump produced by the Dragon GL server into a readable,
color-coded PDF map.

The script is used by the DM commands
.B dm_pdf
and
.B dm_mapfloor
after the server dumps the whole floor to a text file.

Tile colors are kept in sync with the in-game radar minimap
(
.BR src/client/client_minimap.c ,
.I tile_to_color
),
so the PDF matches what the player sees on the 2D top-down view.

The generated PDF contains:
.IP \(bu 2
a title with the floor number and the map dimensions;
.IP \(bu 2
a subtitle with the carved percentage, the number of distinct tile types,
the dump format and the generation time;
.IP \(bu 2
coordinate ticks every 25 tiles along the top and left edges of the map;
.IP \(bu 2
the framed map;
.IP \(bu 2
a legend with a color swatch, the label and the tile count for every tile
type present in the map; the Stairs Up and Stairs Down entries also list
the coordinates of every staircase on the floor;
.IP \(bu 2
a player position marker and a legend entry with the player coordinates,
when the dump carries the player location (v2 dumps only).
.SH DUMP FORMATS
The script auto-detects two dump formats.
.PP
\fBFormat v2 (current).\fR
Written by the current server. A header block is followed by one line per
map row; each tile is its 2-digit hexadecimal
.BR VoxelType
(
.B 00
= unexcavated rock,
.B 1B
= last crystal color, see
.BR include/map.h
):
.PP
.nf
# DragonGL map dump v2
floor 3
width 300
height 300
player 142 97
000001010101...
.fi
.PP
The optional
.B player
line carries the DM position on that floor and is rendered as a marker.
Because every tile type is preserved, the PDF is fully faithful: wood,
cobble, ice, sand, ash, mud, marble, traps, obsidian and all eight crystal
colors are each rendered with their own color.
.PP
\fBFormat v1 (legacy).\fR
A plain grid with one ASCII character per tile and no header, written by
older server versions. Several tile types collapse into a single character
(\&. lumps floor, wood, cobble, ice, sand, ash, mud, marble, traps and six
crystal colors; # lumps wall and obsidian). The PDF is still generated and
a footnote in the legend explains the lumps.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I dump
Path to the floor dump to read (v1 or v2 format).
In v1 dumps all rows are padded to the same width.
.TP
.I output_pdf
Path of the PDF file to create.
.TP
.I floor_number
Optional floor number to display in the PDF title.
If omitted, the value is taken from the dump header (v2) or the dump
filename is searched for a pattern such as
.BR floor_7
or
.BR floor-7 .
If no floor number can be inferred, the label
.B Unknown
is used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-cell " N"
Points per tile in the PDF (default 4.0).
.TP
.BR \-\-px " N"
Internal PNG pixels per tile (default 3).
.TP
.BR \-\-cols " N"
Maximum number of legend columns (default 4).
The layout chooses fewer columns when needed so that labels never overlap.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-coords
Omit the coordinate ticks.
.SH "LEGEND (V1 CHARACTERS)"
The following characters are recognized in v1 dumps:
.TP
.B #
Wall (obsidian in legacy dumps)
.TP
.B +
Door
.TP
.B "\(ti"
Water (labeled Water / Lava when the dump contains no L)
.TP
.B L
Lava
.TP
.B >
Stairs Down
.TP
.B <
Stairs Up
.TP
.B $
Gold Vein
.TP
.B B
Crystal (Blue)
.TP
.B P
Crystal (Purple)
.TP
.B M
Glowing Mushroom
.TP
.B ,
Grass
.TP
.B .
Floor / other carved tiles
.PP
A space character represents unexcavated rock and is rendered with the map
background color.
Any character not explicitly recognized is rendered with the floor color
and listed in the legend as Unknown.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The script requires Python 3 and ReportLab.
.PP
When Pillow is available (ReportLab 4.x requires it), the map is rendered
as a PNG and embedded in the PDF.
With older ReportLab versions where Pillow is optional and missing, the
script falls back to drawing the tiles directly as vector rectangles
(horizontal runs of equal color are merged).
.PP
On Fedora Linux, the required Python packages can be installed with:
.PP
.nf
python3 -m pip install --user reportlab pillow
.fi
.PP
Alternatively, install distribution packages when available:
.PP
.nf
sudo dnf install python3-reportlab python3-pillow
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. The PDF was written successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred. The error description is written to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
The command-line arguments are invalid.
.SH ERRORS
The script exits with a non-zero status when:
.IP \(bu 2
the command-line arguments are invalid (status 2);
.IP \(bu 2
the input dump cannot be read or is empty;
.IP \(bu 2
a v2 dump is malformed (missing header line, wrong grid row length);
.IP \(bu 2
a required Python dependency is missing; or
.IP \(bu 2
the PDF cannot be generated or written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a PDF and infer the floor number from the dump filename:
.PP
.nf
./generate_pdf_map.py map_dump_floor_7.txt floor_7.pdf
.fi
.PP
Explicitly specify the floor number:
.PP
.nf
./generate_pdf_map.py map_dump_floor_7.txt floor_7.pdf 7
.fi
.PP
Use a smaller tile size and fewer legend columns:
.PP
.nf
./generate_pdf_map.py map_dump_floor_7.txt floor_7.pdf 7 \-\-cell 3 \-\-cols 6
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I map_dump.txt , map_dump_floor_*.txt
Floor dump files written by
.BR dm_pdf (1)
and
.BR dm_mapfloor (1)
(v2 format from the current server).
The script does not require a particular filename; the filename is used to
infer the floor number when the third argument is omitted.
.SH NOTES
The color palette is intended to remain synchronized with the mapping used
by
.BR src/client/client_minimap.c ,
and the v2 dump carries the exact
.BR VoxelType
of every tile, so the PDF faithfully reproduces the floor.
.PP
The generated PDF uses a single page whose dimensions are calculated from
the dimensions of the input grid.
